{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`scripts/mutation_gate.py` isolates the **source tree** (`git archive` into a temp\ndir) but not **runtime effects**: `execute_shard()` (line 563) runs pytest as a\nsubprocess against the real filesystem. On 2026-08-26 a mutation removed a guard from\na shell script that performs real deletion, a test exec'd that script with an empty\npath variable, and the glob expanded to `rm -rf /*` — destroying `/opt/homebrew` and\nevery Homebrew-installed app in `/Applications` on a contributor workstation. Only a\n60-second subprocess timeout stopped it; `/Users` was still ahead of it in the target\nlist.\n\n## Why source-tree isolation was never going to help\n\nResearched across the major tools — cargo-mutants, mutmut, StrykerJS, PIT, mull.\n**Every one isolates the source tree; none isolates runtime effects.** A copied tree\ndoes nothing when the mutated code calls `rm -rf /` — the syscall still lands.\ncargo-mutants documents this hazard explicitly\n(https://mutants.rs/cautions.html): *\"it might modify existing deletion paths in your\ncode… could potentially delete unintended directories… Run in an isolated environment\n(container, CI, or VM).\"* No mutation tool in any language ships OS-level sandboxing;\nit is the caller's job.\n\n## Why guards inside the script are not enough\n\nThe harness string-substitutes the artifact under test, so **any guard expressed in\nthat artifact can be deleted by a mutation** — including `${dir:?}`. `${dir:?}` is\nworth adding (statement-removal operators, the most common kind, won't naturally\nrewrite an `rm` argument), but it is hardening, not a control. **Only a layer outside\nthe mutated artifact is load-bearing.** That is this ticket.\n\nNote also that `set -euo pipefail` would NOT have fired: `set -u` triggers on *unset*,\nnot set-but-empty, and the variable was `\"\"`. Verified.\n\n## Scope\n\n1. **Sandbox the mutation run** — the load-bearing control.\n2. **A durable guard against destructive test-exec** — the general form of \"the test\n   should never have been able to delete anything\". The specific offending test is not\n   on `main` (it lives on the unmerged `fix-mcp-clone-blocks-boot` branch), so a\n   one-file fix has nothing to attach to here; a lint gate in the existing\n   `scripts/check_*.py` family generalizes it and catches the next one.\n\nOut of scope: hardening `mcp-entrypoint.sh` itself (`${dir:?}` + deny-list) — that\nfile exists only on the unmerged branch and belongs with Gerrit change 2244.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `execute_shard()` runs its pytest subprocess inside an OS-enforced sandbox that\n      denies filesystem writes outside an explicit allow-list (scratch tree, pytest\n      basetemp, the venv, `/dev/null`). Verified by `pytest tests/unit/test_mutation_sandbox.py`.\n- [ ] The sandbox is fail-closed: with no sandbox mechanism available the run ABORTS\n      with an actionable message naming the opt-out. Verified by a test asserting a\n      non-zero exit when the mechanism is stubbed absent.\n- [ ] A documented opt-out env var waives the sandbox and logs at WARNING when used.\n      A silent unsandboxed fallback is rejected. Verified by asserting the WARNING\n      record is emitted.\n- [ ] Portable per `project.portability`: the mechanism is an OS facility (macOS\n      Seatbelt `sandbox-exec`; a Linux equivalent such as `bwrap`/`unshare`), never a\n      CI-provider feature. Verified by running `make lint` and the sandbox test on a\n      developer host with no CI involved.\n- [ ] The sandbox demonstrably DENIES: a probe writing outside the allow-list under\n      the wrapper exits non-zero and the file is absent; the same probe inside the\n      allow-list succeeds. Asserting wrapper argv alone does not satisfy this — the\n      test must observe enforcement.\n- [ ] A lint gate flags a test that `subprocess`-execs a shell script containing an\n      unguarded destructive deletion, and passes when routed through an injectable\n      seam such as `\"${RM_CMD:-rm}\"`. Verified by\n      `python scripts/check_destructive_test_exec.py`.\n- [ ] That gate runs in `make lint`, consistent with `check_raw_git_writes.py` and\n      `check_wall_clock_asserts.py`. Verified by `make lint`.\n- [ ] `HOME` is set to a non-existent path for the mutation subprocess so adjacent\n      home-directory writes fail loudly. Verified by asserting the child's `HOME`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/unit/test_mutation_sandbox.py` — enforcement (deny outside the\n  allow-list, permit inside), fail-closed abort, and the opt-out WARNING.\n- `pytest tests/unit/test_check_destructive_test_exec.py` — the lint gate flags an\n  unguarded `subprocess`-exec of a destructive script and passes a seamed one.\n- `make lint` — the new gate is wired and the tree is clean.\n- `make typecheck` and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` — no new\n  complexity or module-size violations.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- AC 4's enforcement test passes and fails correctly when the sandbox is disabled.\n- AC 5's gate is in `make lint` with a covering test.\n- No module-size violation: `mutation_gate.py` is **762 LOC against the 800 hard cap**,\n  so the sandbox logic lands in its own module rather than inline.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the complexity ratchet stay green.\n- Operator note documents the opt-out and its risk.\n\n## Notes\n\nFiled from the 2026-08-26 incident. The two other leaks found that day —\ngate-snapshot accumulation (`a37c-d55c-72c3-439b`) and orphaned CPU load generators\n(`f3d9-ecd8-df72-4fa1`) — are unrelated in cause and tracked separately."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787797934421642001,"uuid":"fe4aacc4-c3a8-4a6e-a1e8-796d0d1d63eb"}